body:
> For example, a small code snippet that runs a single way in all browsers, but which the spec would allow to run a different way?

The below snippet is actually an example of something that is already not interoperable. 

In Firefox `70 `, it consistently prints `Message (2): Msg1`, in Chrome `76`, it consistently prints `Message(1): Msg1`. (in Servo it would also print `Message (2): Msg1`).

The observed difference could be due task-queue prioritization, although for example in Servo it would never print `Message(1): Msg1`, due to how one deals with the port message queue while the port is in transfer. The question is if that is also the case in other implementation, and if this points to an actual issue.


```javascript
var channel1 = new MessageChannel();
var channel2 = new MessageChannel();

channel1.port1.postMessage("Transfer 1", [channel2.port1]);
channel2.port2.postMessage("Msg 1");

channel1.port2.onmessage = function(evt) {
  evt.ports[0].onmessage = function(evt) {
    console.log("Message (1):" + evt.data);
  }
  setTimeout(function(){
    evt.target.postMessage("Transfer 2", evt.ports);
  }, 0);
}

channel1.port1.onmessage = function(evt) {
  evt.ports[0].onmessage = function(evt) {
    console.log("Message (2):" + evt.data);
  }
}
```

Note that both printing `Message(1): Msg1` or `Message(2): Msg1` would be spec-compliant, since setting the `onmessage` handler a blocking call to wait for incoming messages, hence any queued message do not have to be delivered before the timeout task in which the port is re-transferred. 

The reason why for example in Servo `Message (2)` would print, is because when the port is transfer-received we would start an asynchronous "complete the port transfer" workflow meant to allow the port message queue(which is an actual queue that is added to a data holder and taken out of it as part of a transfer) to catch-up on messages sent while in transfer. Since that workflow allows the event-loop to keep running, the port will be transferred again before it can complete, and the workflow is aborted. Then in the second transfer-receive steps, we start that workflow again, this time it is able to complete and therefore messages are plugged into the local event-loop. 

The fact that Chrome prints `Message(1): Msg1` points to me to a sync workflow happening when the port is transfer-received, which perhaps blocks the event-loop while completing, and then makes it possible to immediately start dispatching events on the port once it is enabled. 

